  "context_pill": "Tax Liability Calculation (Default Regime)",
  "example_summary": "Mr. X has a total income of ₹ 16,00,000 for A.Y. 2026-27. We need to calculate his tax liability under the default regime of Section 115BAC.",
  "source_provision": "### Statutory Matrix: Default Tax Regime Slabs u/s 115BAC\n\n| Income Slab (₹) | Applicable Tax Rate |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Up to ₹ 4,00,000** | Nil |\n| **₹ 4,00,001 to ₹ 8,00,000** | 5% |\n| **₹ 8,00,001 to ₹ 12,00,000** | 10% |\n| **₹ 12,00,001 to ₹ 16,00,000** | 15% |\n| **₹ 16,00,001 to ₹ 20,00,000** | 20% |\n| **₹ 20,00,001 to ₹ 24,00,000** | 25% |\n| **Above ₹ 24,00,000** | 30% |\n\n### Core Statutory Checklist\n\n* **Default Application:** Section 115BAC is the default tax regime for Individuals, HUFs, AOPs, BOIs, and AJPs unless specifically opted out.\n* **Rebate u/s 87A:** Resident individuals with Total Income up to ₹ 7,00,000 are eligible for a maximum rebate of ₹ 25,000 (with marginal relief for income slightly exceeding ₹ 7,00,000).\n* **Health and Education Cess:** A mandatory levy of 4% applies to the total Income Tax computed before cess.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] Opting Out Mechanism u/s 115BAC(6): Assessees without business income can opt out on a year-to-year basis at the time of filing return u/s 139(1). However, assessees with business/professional income get only one lifetime option to opt out and one option to re-enter.",
